{"qid":"Q711","names":["Mongolia"],"title":"Mongolia","endonym":{"native":"Монгол Улс","lang":"mn"},"capsule":"Mongolia as a sovereign democratic republic emerged from the collapse of the Soviet-era Mongolian People's Republic, adopting a new democratic constitution on 13 January 1992 that established a multiparty parliamentary system and a market economy. The transition brought severe economic contraction (the 'zud' hardships of the 1990s), but Mongolia subsequently developed into one of the most open democracies in Asia, with peaceful transfers of power between the Mongolian People's Party and the Democratic Party. The country's economy expanded rapidly after 2000 driven by large-scale copper and coal extraction — the Oyu Tolgoi copper-gold mine, among the world's largest, reached first production in 2013. Mongolia's landlocked position between Russia and China has constrained economic diversification, and the country continues to pursue a 'third neighbor' foreign policy seeking engagement with the United States, Japan, and South Korea.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Borders are internationally recognized and surveyed; the southern boundary with China and northern boundary with Russia are delimited by bilateral treaties and present no ambiguity for cartographic purposes.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Britannica: Mongolia","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Mongolia"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Mongolia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mongolia"}],"britannica":"place/Mongolia","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Mongolia","rulers":[{"qid":"Q1171817","name":"Dashiin Byambasüren","start":1990,"end":1